      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Several studies demonstrated the feasibility of minimally invasive parathyroidectomy as a treatment for primary hyperparathyroidism. We compared its results with those of traditional surgery in a prospective randomized study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "From March to November 1998, 38 patients eligible for video-assisted parathyroidectomy (VAP) were referred to us. They were randomly divided into 2 groups: patients of group A underwent a conventional cervicotomy with bilateral exploration and frozen section of the removed adenoma; patients of group B underwent VAP with intraoperative measurement of parathyroid hormone. Operative time, postoperative pain, fever and hypocalcemia, cosmetic result, and costs were compared. Two cases of VAP were performed with locoregional anesthesia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Groups A (18 patients) and B (20 patients) were statistically balanced. Operative time was significantly shorter in group B (57 vs 70 minutes). Cosmetic result was significantly better in group B, which also experienced less postoperative pain (P < .05). No cases of persistent primary hyperparathyroidism were present in either group, but recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy occurred in 1 patient in group B."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Compared with conventional surgery, VAP is associated with a shorter operative time, a better cosmetic result, and a less painful postoperative course."
        }
      ]},
